      <h1>⭐ Morning Briefing — Texas &amp; DFW AI Data Centers, Grid, Land Use</h1>
      <p class="briefing-subtitle">(Latest updates through August 10, 2026)</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏗️ 1. Data Center Construction &amp; Corporate Activity</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Dallas-Based Mousterian and Samsung Heavy Industries Move Toward the First Floating AI Data Center in the U.S.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Dallas-based Mousterian Corporation and South Korea's Samsung Heavy Industries signed an engineering contract advancing their planned moored, floating data center (FDC) — the partnership's first U.S. deployment — with a pilot site set to sit adjacent to an existing combined-cycle gas plant near Houston (Data Center Knowledge, Aug. 4; PR Newswire, Aug. 4; Smart Maritime Network, Aug. 4).</li>
        <li>Each floating unit is designed to deliver 50 megawatts of critical IT capacity using bulk non-evaporative cooling, which the companies say requires no potable water and produces no process-water discharge (The Register, Aug. 5).</li>
        <li>The design leans on locating next to power plants that already have grid and cooling-water access, letting a floating campus bypass both a new interconnection request and a new water permit — the two friction points currently driving Gov. Abbott's audit and DFW cities' zoning fights.</li>
        <li>Mousterian and Samsung say further deployments are planned for other U.S. markets beyond the Houston pilot; no DFW-area site has been named.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> A Dallas company just proposed sidestepping both of Texas's biggest 2026 data-center fights — the grid queue and the water permit — by putting the data center on a barge next to a plant that already has both.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏛️ 2. Land-Use &amp; Zoning Developments</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Fort Worth Council Gets Specifics Ahead of Tomorrow's Three-Item Vote</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Fort Worth's City Council takes up three distinct data-center items at its Aug. 11 meeting: a proposed 90-day moratorium on new development, creation of a standing data-center review committee, and new mandatory filing requirements for operators.</li>
        <li>The filing requirements under consideration would require water-efficient cooling technology, regular reporting of electric and water usage, and proof that a project won't raise electric costs for existing Fort Worth ratepayers.</li>
        <li>Also on the table: repealing local sales-tax exemptions and development incentives currently available to data-center projects — a notable reversal from the abatement packages DFW cities have used to attract these facilities.</li>
        <li>Because the Zoning Commission voted 7-4 in July to recommend denial of the city's original ordinance, any of Tuesday's measures need a three-fourths council supermajority — at least 9 of 11 votes — to pass.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Fort Worth's Tuesday vote isn't just a pause anymore — pairing a moratorium with a proposal to strip data centers' sales-tax breaks would be one of the sharpest incentive reversals any Texas city has floated this year.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <p>Quiet elsewhere today: ERCOT and the PUC posted no new interconnection or Batch Zero filings beyond what's already on the record heading into the PUCT's Aug. 20 open meeting, no new TCEQ draft permit or public notice surfaced in any of the eight DFW-area counties this briefing tracks, and no fresh on-site or behind-the-meter generation deal beyond previously reported projects was confirmed as newly dated.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📍 3. Today's Watch List</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Actionable Signals for Monday, August 10</p>

      <h3>ERCOT</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Whether ERCOT or PUCT post any new Batch Zero classification notice ahead of the Aug. 20 open meeting</li>
        <li>Any additional detail on the scope of Gov. Abbott's ERCOT/PUCT audit</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>PUC</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>PUCT's ruling on ERCOT's expected good-cause exception request at the Aug. 20 meeting</li>
        <li>Whether commissioners set a public-comment window for the broader data-center audit</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>TCEQ</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Any new draft permit or public-notice filing tied to a data center or gas plant in Ellis, Collin, Johnson, Denton, Wise, Tarrant, Parker, or Hood counties</li>
        <li>Whether TCEQ is formally looped into the PUCT/ERCOT audit process</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>County Zoning</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Outcome of Fort Worth's Aug. 11 vote on the moratorium, review committee, and filing-requirement package</li>
        <li>Denton's Aug. 18 meeting to set public-hearing dates for its own proposed moratorium</li>
        <li>Whether any other DFW-area city follows Fort Worth or Denton's lead this week</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Corporate Filings</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Whether Mousterian/Samsung name a DFW-area site for a future floating data-center deployment</li>
        <li>Any additional permitting detail tied to Amazon's Pecos County GW Ranch buildout</li>
      </ul>

